WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:10.560
- I'm not normally embarrassed
by the extent of my subject

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.393
at the beginning.

00:00:11.393 --> 00:00:15.300
But I can hardly compete with the idea

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.360
of explaining all behavior
and social activity

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.540
from fundamental axioms of physics.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.630
So I'm gonna focus on
a much smaller problem,

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.977
which is trying to
understand the visual system

00:00:26.977 --> 00:00:28.170
in the mouse. 

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:31.950
And I'll motivate this through
the course of this talk,

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.330
but it actually comes from

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.810
a deeply held philosophical
belief about research,

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.630
which is, if you can
choose the right problem,

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.990
and dig into it deeply enough,

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:46.560
then you're likely to learn lessons

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.210
that will inspire the solution
to other related problems,

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.110
and then in fact,

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:56.520
help you even to be
able to formulate them.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.670
And so for those of you
who don't care about

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.680
biological visual systems,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.600
I've changed the intro to the talk

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.780
based on several of the
comments that I heard yesterday

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.770
and today, which is about
the rise of AI in science,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.020
and in our culture in general.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.500
So Priya, this is my
attempt to get large again.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.470
And the question is
whether these artifacts

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.456
are actually doing things

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:25.890
like this other intelligent
machine that we know about,

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.043
at least at the level
of our visual systems.

00:01:29.043 --> 00:01:32.720
And as I'm sure everyone
in this room knows,

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.720
deep networks came out
of a very crude analogy

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:43.120
with a very crude model
from the late 1970s

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.364
by David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel

00:01:47.364 --> 00:01:51.540
modeling the early
visual system in the cat.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.780
And this model has now been extended

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.780
into the foundation of much
of the deep learning aspect

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.320
of modern AI.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.050
And there is in fact a something

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.270
of a Manhattan project going on

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:13.470
that wants to build an identity
between these deep networks

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.721
and the structure at
least of visual systems.

00:02:16.721 --> 00:02:20.910
And frankly, I was struck a
lot by your comment yesterday

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.130
about whether we're writing papers

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.950
that are only to be read by machine.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:30.950
And that takes this actually
to a much higher level.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.410
Is the nature of this intelligence

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.800
anything like what we
would call intelligence?

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.170
Like, I don't know the
answer to the big question,

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.940
but I'm gonna try to address that,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.720
at least at the level
of the visual system.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.840
And, so how might you go about this?

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.277
And my goal here is to set the stage

00:02:51.277 --> 00:02:55.410
and introduce our
algorithmic approach to this.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:59.460
Well, there's some unknown
neural network in say,

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:00.930
a visual system,

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:05.730
and we excite it with some
stimulus of one sort or another.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.480
And we record the activity,
we measure the activity,

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.610
and I believe the
philosophers in this room

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.754
would call that an experiment.

00:03:13.754 --> 00:03:17.100
And the question is, once
you do an experiment,

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.713
what do you do with all those data?

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.650
In our case, we're going
to deal with the mouse

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:29.550
because it is the system of choice

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.010
for much of modern biology.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:37.010
And so these mice are
pretty good at wandering

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.930
through the world and surviving.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:44.310
And so there's sort of
three questions, which are,

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.770
what are the stimuli that we should use

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.830
to excite the visual system of the mouse

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.750
in a non-trivial fashion?

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.530
And how do we build some notion

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.110
of what the circuits might be

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.200
that are underlying that activity?

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.090
And we would like to be able to do this

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:07.530
by analyzing the data that we
acquire through experiments.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.220
Now, there's of something of
a history to this subject,

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:14.444
which is very anti-mouse, which,

00:04:14.444 --> 00:04:17.760
if you take the mouse and
put it in the laboratory,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.120
as we would all go to the ophthalmologist

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.730
and measure the acuity of these animals,

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.910
it turns out that mice
are effectively blind.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.770
That is to say,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.650
if I asked how well you
could see something small,

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:35.040
like say your thumbnail,
held at arm's length,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.630
say your thumbnail at arm's length

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.240
is about one degree of visual angle.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.290
And the acuity of a mouse
with respect to that

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:47.880
is only less than 10%
as good as being able

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.800
to see something like your thumbnail.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.080
So if a primate were viewing a scene

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.930
like the one that you
see here on the right,

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.300
then it would see a pattern

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.730
like this blurry mess over there.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:04.730
So this says that maybe the
mouse is not the right creature

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.600
to be using for visual experiments,

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:10.433
despite the fact that

00:05:10.433 --> 00:05:15.210
there's so much known about it
genetically and biologically

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.479
and so many imaging capabilities.

00:05:17.479 --> 00:05:19.590
But if you go down a level,

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.220
you see that the neural
machinery in the mouse

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.060
is very analogous to the
neural machinery in cats

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.754
as Hubel and Wiesel used
them, or in the primate.

00:05:29.754 --> 00:05:34.050
Maybe there's something
else that's going on here

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.110
that requires a little
bit of understanding.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:38.040
And that is,

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.330
is the stimulus that your
ophthalmologist would use

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.070
to design your glasses,

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.560
the right kind of stimulus to be using

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.740
in evaluating the visual
system of the mouse?

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.330
So we thought about that,

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:55.590
and from a mathematician's
perspective, what do mice do?

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:56.970
Well, they run around in fields,

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.280
looking for crickets
and other things to eat.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.220
So if you picture yourself as a mouse

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.630
running through a field,

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:06.330
there's lots of grass
and shrubbery and whatnot

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:09.210
at the scale that matters to you.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.490
And so as you're running
through the field,

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.730
you see this kind of
waterfall of shrubbery

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.890
and grass flowing by.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:20.070
And this led us, as mathematicians,

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:24.000
to build a collection of flow patterns

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.000
that are an abstraction of
that class of visual stimuli.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.910
And my colleague, Michael Stryker,

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.670
has used these flow patterns

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:39.180
to assess the visual system of the mouse.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.060
And there's a key data science point here,

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.510
which is the flows are
much more naturalistic

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:49.170
than the artificial stimuli

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:51.450
that you see on the left
side of your screen.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:54.450
But they're also much
more amenable to analysis

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.190
than natural images,

00:06:56.190 --> 00:07:00.487
which are essentially an
uncontrolled class of stimuli

00:07:00.487 --> 00:07:02.370
that you see on the right.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.130
So to get the experiments to work,

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.910
you need to be somewhat
cognizant of the stimuli.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.137
Now, how are these experiments done?

00:07:11.137 --> 00:07:13.680
We're the mathematicians.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.220
This is all done by my colleague,
Michael Stryker, at UCSF.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.470
And the game is played as follows.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.510
We need to record from the mouse

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.430
while the mouse is running around

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:27.030
in what it thinks of as
a naturalistic situation,

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:31.650
looking at these flow patterns
going by on computer screens.

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:35.880
And this is done with a very clever scheme

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:37.500
developed by David Tank,

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.500
in which you bolt the mouse's
head to a fixed object,

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.260
so you can insert
electrodes to do recordings.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.250
But at the same time,

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:50.790
the mouse is standing
on a ball of styrofoam,

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:54.240
which is elevated by a jet of air.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.980
And the moment of inertia
of the styrofoam ball's

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.180
the same as the mouse.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.070
So the mouse is running
around on this ball,

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.780
looking at our computer screens.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.100
We can record from his brain
because it's immobilized,

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.260
but the mouse thinks he's
running around through the world,

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:14.070
or at least that's our sense
of what we think is going on.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:16.050
Now, with that setup,

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.440
we can evaluate the response
to these flow patterns.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.840
And this becomes a little
bit technical here,

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.480
and I'm trying to not be
technical in this talk.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.960
But you can see from this rendition

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:30.120
of what are called the receptive fields

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:33.540
of the visual neurons
in the mouse's brain,

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:36.150
that there's a lot of
structure to the flow

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.280
that's at a much higher frequency

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.490
than the size of these receptive fields.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.307
And here the red lobe
indicates an excitatory,

00:08:44.307 --> 00:08:47.610
and the blue lobe indicates
an inhibitory portion

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:51.090
of the visual field that
the mouse cares about.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:55.860
And to make a non-technical observation

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:59.640
of the summary of the data,
mice love these scenes.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.040
They love these visual flow stimuli.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.710
They respond at two and a half,

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:07.277
maybe three octaves higher in frequency

00:09:07.277 --> 00:09:09.780
to these artificial stimuli.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.200
Contrast matters a lot,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.550
And in fact, if you look at
the cells in mouse cortex

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:19.200
that care only about the gradings,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.440
about the classic stimuli,
it's a small percentage.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.380
And so, this is lesson number one.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:28.890
You need to be very
careful about making sure

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:31.050
that you're exercising the system

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:35.550
at least in a non-trivial way
to the problems that you have.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:38.820
The second is a data science problem,

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:43.560
which is once we have
this ensemble of stimuli,

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:46.380
the analysis question
becomes much more difficult,

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:51.180
which is, how to analyze the
response of a visual system

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.450
to an entire ensemble of stimuli?

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:58.200
Now we have recordings from, let's say,

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:03.200
1,000 neurons to each of the
stimuli in this ensemble,

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.060
in this behaving situation.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:10.770
And so there is a popular
approach to analyzing this.

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:13.423
And this is right at the
heart of what's going on

00:10:13.423 --> 00:10:18.150
in the bulk of computational
neuroscience these days,

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.390
where you show the mouse the stimulus,

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:23.790
you record from 1,000 neurons,

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:28.260
you measure the response to
neuron one, to stimulus one,

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:32.610
the response of neuron two to
stimulus two, dot, dot, dot.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.430
The response of neuron 1,000 to,

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:37.740
and you do that for each of the stimuli.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.148
It's a high dimensional space.

00:10:39.148 --> 00:10:42.750
You reduce dimensionality
with traditional techniques

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:46.950
like PCA and you get what's
called a perceptual manifold.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:49.650
Now, the key thing about
this perceptual manifold

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:54.650
is each point is a stimulus,
and they're in coordinates

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:57.750
that relate to the activity of mice.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:01.290
The principle components of the stimulus.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:04.440
Key thing, each dot in this manifold,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:07.465
each point on this manifold, is a trial.

00:11:07.465 --> 00:11:11.760
So if you imagine yourself
walking around on this,

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.260
as you walk on the manifold,

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.870
you're walking trial to trial to trial.

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:17.850
What the manifold is telling you about

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.040
is in the context of the brain,

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.800
how do the trials relate to one another?

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.030
Well, this is extremely useful
if your job is to decode

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.730
what you think the mouse was looking at.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:31.620
And for that reason,

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:36.620
these kinds of techniques are
used a lot in brain implants

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:38.700
and other such things,

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:42.783
where you want to decode a
stimulus from the activity.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.390
In understanding the
visual system, however,

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:50.070
we're not out to build a prosthesis.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.502
We're out to figure out
what the circuits are.

00:11:52.502 --> 00:11:57.150
These manifolds describe
which trials are similar.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.450
What we wanna know is what
are the neurons doing?

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:01.620
That is to say,

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:04.740
we need something like the
inverse of that manifold.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:09.740
So instead of representing
trials in neural coordinates,

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:13.710
we need to represent
neurons in coordinates

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.840
that relate not only to the trials,

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:18.450
but to the individual responses.

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:22.200
And we've developed an approach
to building this manifold,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.870
and we call it the
neural encoding manifold,

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:27.060
'cause it's in a sense,

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:31.260
the inverse to the
standard decoding manifold.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.360
The key point on this manifold

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:37.710
is neurons live in neighborhoods

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:42.710
where they respond similarly
to the stimulus ensemble

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:46.050
in time to the other neurons around them.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.900
So neurons nearby on this manifold

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:51.930
are likely to be involved
in functional circuits.

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.920
Neurons far away are doing
something rather different.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.940
And the hope is that once
we understand this manifold,

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:03.090
it'll tell us something about
abstract functional circuitry,

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:06.480
which can then tell us
something about real circuitry.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:10.440
So here's the way it looks with real data.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:13.710
This is the Stryker setup at UCSF,

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:16.122
but because as I described it,

00:13:16.122 --> 00:13:20.490
the visual system in the
mouse responds so surprisingly

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.530
to these flow stimuli

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:24.120
that we thought we'd better take a look

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.030
at the input to the cortex.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:31.170
And so we teamed up with Greg
Field, who's now at UCLA,

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:36.060
and so we have exactly
analogous presentations

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:39.960
of our stimulus ensemble to the cortex

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:44.955
and to the retina of mice
under comparable conditions.

00:13:44.955 --> 00:13:49.955
And that's the siloing
effect that we'll see.

00:13:53.004 --> 00:13:55.860
So the stimulus ensemble is

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:57.750
of course all the classic stimuli

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:00.600
because we need to relate to the activity

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.970
of the rest of the neuroscience world,

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:06.750
but also a collection of these
flow stimuli that represent

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:10.050
what a mouse would be doing in
a more naturalistic setting.

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:12.750
And they're designed so
that we can titrate out

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:15.840
various aspects of stimulus properties

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:20.460
that we think the mouse
would be interested in.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:24.390
This is a real version of
that decoding manifold,

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:25.650
the classic approach.

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:28.500
And you can see from this
that the different groups

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:31.800
of stimuli form clouds in that space.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.990
So it would be relatively trivial

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:35.743
from a machine learning perspective

00:14:35.743 --> 00:14:38.910
to design decoding algorithms to say

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:41.100
what stimulus the mouse was looking at.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:43.140
You see the beautiful clustering here

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:45.217
in visual cortex of the mouse.

00:14:45.217 --> 00:14:48.153
Well, what does it look
like in the retina?

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:53.190
The clusters are even more
separate. The margins are bigger.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.590
The decoding problem is easier.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:57.720
So if you want it to solve
this kind of problem,

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:00.722
you're better off using
a retina than a brain.

00:15:00.722 --> 00:15:03.030
So what's going on in the brain?

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:06.270
The brain is already doing
something much more complicated,

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:09.337
which seems to make that
classical decoding issue

00:15:09.337 --> 00:15:11.880
a rather different one.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:15.360
So how we will we approach this?

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.459
Let me just familiarize you
with the way we present,

00:15:18.459 --> 00:15:21.210
I will be presenting the data.

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:26.210
And that is think of a
particular retinal ganglion cell,

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:30.030
and it sees a bunch of stimuli drifting by

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.470
in different directions.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:33.090
Some of these are grading patterns,

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:35.430
some of these are flows of various sorts.

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:38.220
And so for each one of
these, we get a map,

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:41.430
which is the direction of flow

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:45.270
versus the time since
the stimulus came on.

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:48.930
And you can see here that
if this particular neuron

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:52.110
was selective for this
region of visual space,

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:56.823
as the stimulus came by,
it would brrr, quiet,

00:15:56.823 --> 00:15:59.730
brrr, quiet, brrr, quiet.

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:02.790
And you could see that as
in one of these directions

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:06.570
of flow, brr, quiet, brrr, quiet, brr.

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:09.270
So red means lots of activity.

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:11.610
Black means not much activity.

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:15.540
You get a similar kind of
picture in the visual cortex,

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:17.730
except now it's a little
bit more structured

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:18.960
in visual cortex,

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:22.216
neurons tend to care about
whether it's drifting this way

00:16:22.216 --> 00:16:24.600
with a grading of that orientation,

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:28.230
or drifting this way with a
grading of that orientation.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.780
And so you get the same
kind of distribution

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:34.500
of responses here, but a
bit more refined, okay?

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:36.720
This is two classic examples,

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:39.570
you could find cells
exhibiting this behavior

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:41.100
in the Hubel, Wiesel experiments,

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:43.470
that I talked about at the beginning.

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:48.270
What happens if you show the
entire ensemble of six stimuli?

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:49.650
And that's, I'm going through this.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:51.780
So you get familiar with thinking

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:54.270
of the different stimulus class

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:58.950
as one of the positions
in this array of activity

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:00.330
for each of the cells.

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:03.030
Well, that cell in V1 that I showed

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:05.370
with the classical Hubel, Wiesel response,

00:17:05.370 --> 00:17:08.220
didn't care about any
of the other stimuli.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:10.170
That's a very classical cell.

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:13.680
Those are the ones on which
deep networks are based.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:17.790
Most cells don't respond that
way in the visual cortex.

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:20.370
Most cells, for example, this one,

00:17:20.370 --> 00:17:22.890
has no interest in low frequency gradings

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:26.310
and has beautiful responses
tuned to orientation

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:28.380
to high frequency gradings,

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:32.760
or to flow patterns that
have orientation structure.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:35.520
It doesn't like un-oriented,

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:39.870
or just dot kinetic motion patterns at all

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:43.290
unless the contrast is negative, okay?

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:47.130
What's going on here? Here's
another retinal ganglion cell.

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:48.960
This is the one I showed
at the beginning, right?

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:51.270
There's those bursts of activity

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:54.150
as the low frequency grading drifted by.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:56.490
It loves high frequency gradings.

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:58.710
It loves positive contrast flows.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:02.133
It doesn't respond at all to
negative contrast flows, right?

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:04.470
What's going on here?

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:08.460
Every neuron is giving a
response in different ways

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:10.410
to this ensemble of stimuli.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:15.410
This is a pretty complicated
system to begin to understand.

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:20.750
That's why we're trying to
build this encoding manifold

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:22.286
so that we can begin to understand

00:18:22.286 --> 00:18:26.940
how these properties are
represented in the visual system.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:29.190
Now, the heart of the
matter in doing this,

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:32.100
for those of you familiar
with machine learning ideas,

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:35.310
this becomes a very technical
problem in manifold inference.

00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:36.996
I'm gonna skip over all those details,

00:18:36.996 --> 00:18:39.148
other than to give you a sense

00:18:39.148 --> 00:18:42.630
of how the mathematics works behind this.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:47.367
Which is to say we have
a collection of neurons,

00:18:47.367 --> 00:18:50.910
each of which is responding
to an ensemble of stimuli,

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:53.490
and a response is given
as a function in time.

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:56.400
So this is a tensor of data.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:57.397
And the question is,

00:18:57.397 --> 00:19:00.810
"How to say which points
in that tensor of data

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:02.580
are similar to one another?"

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:05.040
Well, points are going to be neurons.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:08.490
So look at, for example,
neuron I over here.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:10.680
It likes low frequency gradings

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:13.680
and it likes negative contrast flows,

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.740
and it has a strong transient aspect

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:18.720
to its response to the flows

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:21.720
that you don't really see
with the gradings, okay?

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:25.260
Notice that neuron I and
neuron K are really similar

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:28.440
to each other in the
response to the ensemble.

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:31.260
And so we can build
what's called a kernel,

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:32.580
which measures that,

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.580
and this is a very
technical aspect of things,

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:38.610
but what it says is neurons I and K

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:42.510
should be close by each
other on this manifold.

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:44.088
Now, neurons I and J,

00:19:44.088 --> 00:19:47.370
which are responding quite differently,

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:51.180
notice that I like the
negative contrast flows.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:52.866
J didn't like those at all.

00:19:52.866 --> 00:19:56.535
J liked positive contrast flows.

00:19:56.535 --> 00:19:59.760
I didn't respond to those at all.

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:03.690
So I and K are close, J is far away.

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:05.790
You build a graph of these data.

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:10.290
Distances in this graph are
given by this similarity kernel.

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:13.170
In the limit as the number
of neurons gets large,

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:16.020
there's a convergence in
a certain technical sense

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:18.030
of that graph to a manifold,

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:21.600
and that's what we're calling
the neural encoding manifold.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.750
Okay? So near each point is a neuron.

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:29.750
Points nearby are responding
similar to the ensemble.

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:33.603
This is what the data really
looks like for the retina.

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:35.333
As I show it,

00:20:35.333 --> 00:20:37.800
while it's projected down
into three dimensions.

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:38.850
But as I show it,

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:42.390
even just projected down
to three dimensions,

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:43.540
you immediately see

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:47.310
that this is a highly
clustered representation

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:48.690
of the neurons.

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:52.124
There are places where
the neurons are very dense

00:20:52.124 --> 00:20:54.300
in this representation,

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:58.284
and the places where it's
dense are separated by areas

00:20:58.284 --> 00:21:02.850
that are pretty empty of neurons.

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:06.408
And so this highly
clustered representation

00:21:06.408 --> 00:21:11.408
for the retina seems to be curious.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:16.490
Remember, every point in this
representation is a neuron.

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.300
So since it's a neuron,

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:20.550
we know it's everything about its response

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:23.040
to our ensemble of stimuli.

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:27.450
Neurons that are in the same
cluster are by definition,

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:29.940
near each other on the manifold,

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.730
these high density areas on the manifold.

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:36.540
So they exhibit similar
responses to similar aspects

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:38.820
of the stimulus ensemble.

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:41.160
If I go to a different cluster,

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:44.340
I find a very different
profile of responses

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:46.020
to our ensemble.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:49.470
If I go to a different
neuron in that cluster,

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:50.670
it looks the same.

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:53.070
So as I walk across this manifold,

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:55.110
I see high density of cells

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:57.240
that are responding similarly, boom.

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:00.600
Then a big jump into cells that
are responding differently.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:04.350
Boom, a big jump into cells
that are responding differently.

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:05.370
And I can keep doing that.

00:22:05.370 --> 00:22:07.110
I can walk over the whole manifold,

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:08.733
and I can keep doing that.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:12.543
Why would you expect that to be the case?

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:18.600
It turns out that these
clusters are not arbitrary.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:22.170
They relate to what's
known about the structure

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:23.340
of the retina,

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:27.120
which is retinal ganglion cells
are organized into classes.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:27.953
In the mouse,

00:22:27.953 --> 00:22:30.960
there's thought to be maybe
up to about 40 such classes.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:33.060
It's thought that in human primates,

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:35.760
there's maybe 20 such classes.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:38.250
In these 40 classes, they have names,

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:41.250
they have characterization,
they have properties.

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:43.410
The neurons in these clusters

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:47.100
are representing all
those properties, okay?

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:52.100
This is not to say that not much was added

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:55.920
to retinal physiology, but I
would like you to read this

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:59.970
as saying this is a
confirmation in some sense,

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.970
that our algorithms are at least
doing something appropriate

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:05.160
with regard to neural activity

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:09.568
because it's learning in a
completely unsupervised fashion

00:23:09.568 --> 00:23:12.270
much of the structure of what was known

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:16.140
about retinal ganglion
cell properties, okay?

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:18.360
V1, visual cortex,

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:21.585
the first area that
processes visual information

00:23:21.585 --> 00:23:24.660
in human primates,

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:27.750
it's in the occipital lobe way back here

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:29.400
in the back of your brain.

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:31.683
And what do these look like?

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:41.250
The manifold for the
cortex is to the extent

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:44.460
that we can characterize
it completely continuous.

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:48.191
You notice there's this beautiful,

00:23:48.191 --> 00:23:50.220
high dimensional structure.

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:52.470
It's hard to tell in this
low dimensional projection.

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:54.660
There's this beautiful
high dimensional structure

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:58.590
with this low dimensional
arm coming out of the side.

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:01.500
There were several years of mathematical

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:03.330
and computational work in front,

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:07.050
trying to develop algorithms
for manifold inference

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:10.230
that work properly in what
are called non-pure manifolds

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:11.063
in mathematics.

00:24:11.063 --> 00:24:12.480
Manifolds, where in it,

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:17.190
where the data change their
local intrinsic dimension.

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:20.400
How will we characterize
these continuous manifolds?

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:22.140
Well, each point is a neuron.

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:24.150
So we could stand on a neuron,

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:26.850
we could look at its properties,
and then we could start.

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:29.670
So for example, if we stood
on this neuron over here,

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:31.320
we could look at its properties.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:36.300
As we start to walk along a
geodesic on this manifold,

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:39.480
we can encounter other neurons

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:41.550
and they have different properties.

00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:44.760
And you see, as long as
you go along pathway one,

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:48.060
as cartooned in this diagram,

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:51.390
we go from neurons that
prefer all the stimuli,

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:55.290
highly promiscuous with regard
to our stimulus ensemble,

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:58.290
to neurons that really
only like flow patterns.

00:24:58.290 --> 00:25:01.230
And so we go from neurons
over in this corner

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:02.400
that like everything,

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:05.648
to neurons over here that
really only like flows.

00:25:05.648 --> 00:25:08.400
What if we went along the opposite

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:11.490
and opposite direction on this manifold?

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:14.010
We see that, again, the properties change,

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:16.046
but as you walk along that direction,

00:25:16.046 --> 00:25:20.880
what you lose is selectivity
for low frequency gradings,

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:25.200
responsiveness to low frequency gradings.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:27.900
Well, we can characterize many aspects

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:30.420
of the structure of the physiology here.

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:31.767
That's not what this talk is about.

00:25:31.767 --> 00:25:34.050
But I just want you to
get this kind of picture

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:37.560
of how beautifully
continuous this structure is,

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:41.250
and how we can characterize
various aspects of it.

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:42.180
Remember in the beginning,

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:45.030
I talked about the
importance of the stimuli.

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:47.940
The difficulty of working only

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:50.573
with the classical low frequency gradings

00:25:50.573 --> 00:25:54.990
is these are the neurons that
care about those primarily.

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:57.000
It's only this low frequency arm.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:01.170
So a lot of visual
physiology is based on one,

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:05.040
actually quite special
branch of the manifold

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:08.160
of neurons in visual cortex.

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:12.570
These neurons over here on this
side that liked everything,

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:15.840
they're primarily inhibitory
interneurons because it,

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:16.890
and in fact,

00:26:16.890 --> 00:26:20.907
it's known that there are many properties

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:24.270
of inhibitory interneurons

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:26.970
that lead them to respond widely

00:26:26.970 --> 00:26:28.770
to different classes of stimuli.

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:30.420
We can take this on and on,

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:35.395
and we can characterize many
physiological properties

00:26:35.395 --> 00:26:38.490
about the distribution
and densities of neurons

00:26:38.490 --> 00:26:41.190
at different layers, how
they're related to each other.

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:43.342
That's all visual neuroscience.

00:26:43.342 --> 00:26:46.680
Why would you expect this
topological diversity

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:49.980
that we found in these
two manifolds to arise?

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:53.400
This is a real segregation in the nature

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.800
of the computations going on.

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:59.760
Well, one of the areas we
work on in my building a lot

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:00.960
is spectral graph theory.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:04.680
So here's spectral graph
theory in three seconds, okay?

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:06.570
If you have a collection of neurons

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:08.280
that are tightly coupled to each other

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.110
in a functional network,

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:13.950
then the manifold
corresponding to that graph

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:18.060
will be a dense,
continuous little cluster.

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:19.740
If you have several of these circuits

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:22.170
and they're distinct from one another,

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:25.440
then you'll have a discontinuous manifold

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:30.440
represented in the stimulus
response, extrinsic coordinates.

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:35.520
If you start to add some
weak coupling between these,

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:38.280
then some of these
islands start to connect

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:39.600
up to each other a little bit.

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:41.550
So you have a big island structure

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:44.310
with some weak interconnections
in between them.

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:46.890
And we think that's what's
going on in the retina.

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:48.510
The weak interconnections in the retina

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:52.140
are what are called amacrine
cells or gap junctions

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:54.390
between retinal ganglion cells, et cetera.

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:57.180
We know a lot about the
physiology and anatomy

00:27:57.180 --> 00:27:58.800
that could underlie that.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:01.380
In cortex, the game is
completely different.

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:02.940
Zillions of neurons are connected

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:04.740
to zillions of other neurons,

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:08.458
and the situation is much
higher dimensional, much richer,

00:28:08.458 --> 00:28:11.310
much more densely interconnected.

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:13.923
So all this makes a lot of sense.

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:20.220
That's the intro. That's the
science that we care about.

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:23.370
Here's the part that may
be relevant for you, right?

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:27.120
I said at the beginning, we
have these deep, deep networks.

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:28.770
We started to work with visual networks,

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:31.680
but we're, yeah, there's
a lot more to be said.

00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:35.587
So we can now ask this
question, not at the level of,

00:28:35.587 --> 00:28:37.650
"Does a unit in a deep network

00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:41.280
resemble a unit in the retina
or a unit in the brain?

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:45.450
But does the topology
of the encoding manifold

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:49.230
in the artificial network represent

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:50.670
one of these structures?"

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:54.000
Okay? I won't embarrass
anybody by asking. Okay?

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:55.170
So here's what you do.

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:57.457
The first question you're
worried about, I know, is, well,

00:28:57.457 --> 00:29:01.457
"Is AI an appropriate object
for us to be working with?"

00:29:01.457 --> 00:29:05.850
'Cause the fact that we can
technically interrogate it

00:29:05.850 --> 00:29:09.060
exactly the same way that we
interrogate the biological data

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:10.320
isn't so much the point.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:13.090
Is it appropriate? So what
are these deep networks?

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:18.090
This is ResNet-50, a classic
computer vision network.

00:29:18.330 --> 00:29:20.520
It's trained on this famous library

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:23.759
of natural scenes called ImageNet.

00:29:23.759 --> 00:29:25.500
There are millions of them.

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:29.067
And it has this spectacular
ability to classify

00:29:29.067 --> 00:29:34.067
in instances of these scenes
into, for example, spiderwebs,

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:38.160
or one of 1,000 or so other categories.

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:40.950
This has been all over
the cover of Nature.

00:29:40.950 --> 00:29:43.080
It's been all over the
cover of the New York Times.

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:45.180
You all know this story, okay?

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:48.360
So we get feeded our
stimuli. They're just images.

00:29:48.360 --> 00:29:49.980
We feed it our stimuli.

00:29:49.980 --> 00:29:51.900
And if, for example,

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:54.960
we look at the activity across the layers

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:56.610
of one of these networks,

00:29:56.610 --> 00:30:00.090
we see that for ImageNet-like
patterns or our patterns,

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:04.380
we get similar kinds of
activity distributions, okay?

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:05.520
What's the output?

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:10.520
These networks happily classify
our stimuli as, for example,

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:14.731
positive contrast flows are spiderwebs

00:30:14.731 --> 00:30:19.650
and ResNet does that
with 93.5% confidence.

00:30:19.650 --> 00:30:21.739
So we think that's with regard to,

00:30:21.739 --> 00:30:24.540
I don't know if that's a
prediction or a causation,

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:28.124
but that's a pretty confident prediction,

00:30:28.124 --> 00:30:31.803
that's a pretty confident
estimation of one sort or another.

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:34.620
What does the manifold look like?

00:30:36.690 --> 00:30:38.740
It's even more clustered than the retina.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:44.130
Remember the way I showed individual,

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:47.310
so in this case, we call them
units rather than neurons,

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:51.660
because they're the equivalent
in these deep networks.

00:30:51.660 --> 00:30:56.250
If you were to look at the
responses plotted the same way,

00:30:56.250 --> 00:30:57.907
for one of these units, you'd say,

00:30:57.907 --> 00:31:00.750
"Ah, that's pretty similar
to the kinds of units,

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:02.010
some of the kinds of units

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:03.690
that we saw in the visual system."

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:07.950
Do they tile space the way
that retinal ganglion cells do?

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:09.306
Yes, they do.

00:31:09.306 --> 00:31:13.230
There's a beautiful tiling
of space in the same way

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:15.660
that retinal ganglion cells tile space.

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:17.850
But in retrospect, that
shouldn't be a surprise,

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:19.980
because all these tricks that are used

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:23.040
to make these networks
trainable, like weight sharing,

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:27.060
are in fact imposing that as a condition.

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:29.790
So even though individually,

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:32.880
these things kind of look like neurons,

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:37.140
globally, they look and
enjoy the properties

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:42.090
of something more like a
retina than like a brain.

00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:45.723
These are big retinas. These
are not little brains, hmm?

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:48.480
You can go across different layers,

00:31:48.480 --> 00:31:53.480
different networks, et cetera.

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:55.140
Same thing holds.

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:58.170
So what I tried to illustrate

00:31:58.170 --> 00:32:01.050
by taking this deep dive
into the visual system

00:32:01.050 --> 00:32:05.460
is building up an infrastructure

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:10.330
for understanding populations
of neurons' responses

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:16.800
in respect to an ensemble
of stimuli across time.

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:19.740
The full set of data that
you need to worry about

00:32:19.740 --> 00:32:22.200
leads to very different ways
of thinking about things

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:27.030
and opened a direction into
comparing deep networks

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:30.243
with retinas, with V1,

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:33.690
very specific retinal ganglion cell types

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:36.930
look very much like certain of the units

00:32:36.930 --> 00:32:38.370
in these deep networks.

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:41.340
V1 is distributed very, very differently.

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:43.197
V1 is this continuous manifold,

00:32:43.197 --> 00:32:46.230
and we think that it's
that continuous manifold

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:49.470
that forms the substrate out
of which the emergent phenomena

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:50.490
that Priya,

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:55.020
I think would call cognitive
activity can emerge.

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:57.993
That's not the case in the retina.

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:00.120
From these algorithms,

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:01.470
you can compute dimensionality

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:03.420
and you see the lowest dimensional objects

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:05.190
are the ones from the artificial networks.

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:08.280
The intermediate dimension
stuff is the retina,

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:11.460
which has some of this amacrine cell

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:14.280
mediated behavior introduced.

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:18.180
But cortex, even the first visual area

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:21.303
of cortex is drastically different.

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:24.240
This was a big team effort.

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:28.320
We did the computational work here.

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:32.340
Mike's group at UCSF
did the cortical work.

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:38.700
Greg Field was at Duke, now is at UCLA,

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:40.860
did the retina stuff.

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:44.100
And I'd like to just underline the fact

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:47.251
that this was a spectacular collaboration

00:33:47.251 --> 00:33:50.910
between experimentalists
of different breeds

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:53.640
and theoreticians of different breeds.

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:58.020
And we think that's really
the future of all of this.

00:33:58.020 --> 00:33:59.083
Thank you.

00:33:59.083 --> 00:33:59.916
- Thank you so much, Steve.

00:33:59.916 --> 00:34:02.147
(audience applauding)

00:34:02.147 --> 00:34:05.340
Thanks for cracking open one case

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:08.820
where you have managed to decode

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:13.770
what resonant actually does with spatial,

00:34:13.770 --> 00:34:17.640
broken up how the inference
actually happens within ResNet

00:34:17.640 --> 00:34:20.640
by mapping it onto actually retinal cells,

00:34:20.640 --> 00:34:22.470
real physical entities.

00:34:22.470 --> 00:34:25.320
So cracking open that proverbial black box

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:27.150
that we've all talked about.

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:29.880
So a question to Joe, and then start.

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:31.355
- [Joe] Thanks,

00:34:31.355 --> 00:34:32.673
so one of the things I
really liked about this,

00:34:32.673 --> 00:34:36.120
this setup, is the way that you've got,

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:39.240
not just the changing visual flow,

00:34:39.240 --> 00:34:41.580
but also more or less correlated

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:42.840
with the movement of the mouse,

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:44.010
even though the mouse is stable

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:45.660
because of the ball arrangement, right?

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:48.200
But I'm curious whether there's any,

00:34:48.200 --> 00:34:53.200
what the arrangement is for
figuring in the mouse's head

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:57.870
and eye movements, things
which are not constrained,

00:34:57.870 --> 00:34:59.028
I take it,
- Right.

00:34:59.028 --> 00:35:03.960
- Which are clearly part
of the actual tracking

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:05.377
of changing visual,

00:35:05.377 --> 00:35:08.790
your movement in relation
to the movement around

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:10.320
is really important in vision.

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:12.480
So how does that play
into this kind of project?

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:16.950
- Oh, it's a great question.
We worried about that a lot.

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:19.770
And the head is fixed.
- [Joe] Ah.

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:22.320
- But the eyes can move.

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:25.440
So Mike recorded eye movements.

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:26.273
And then Greg,

00:35:26.273 --> 00:35:29.550
who did an exogenous recording
with pieces of retina,

00:35:29.550 --> 00:35:32.940
moved the stimulus around
as if it were being

00:35:32.940 --> 00:35:35.117
seen through those eye movements.

00:35:35.117 --> 00:35:37.704
- [Joe] Ah. So that correlated
to the eye movements?

00:35:37.704 --> 00:35:39.007
That's really cool.

00:35:39.007 --> 00:35:40.560
- And in fact, we used
the actual eye movement.

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:43.033
- [Joe] Oh yeah, that would
be the point. Yeah. Yeah.

00:35:46.843 --> 00:35:49.380
- [Audience Member] Hi,
thanks for all the great talk.

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:50.640
So could you repeat, I missed it.

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:54.540
How did you pick three as
the embedding dimension

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:56.460
of the lower dimensional manifold?

00:35:56.460 --> 00:35:58.380
And also,
- How did we pick three?

00:35:58.380 --> 00:36:01.942
Oh, oh, oh, because three
makes for a nice video.

00:36:01.942 --> 00:36:05.220
I don't really know how to
display four dimensions at once.

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:07.032
- [Audience Member] And
also, did you, like...

00:36:07.032 --> 00:36:09.450
- We know a lot about
the intrinsic dimension

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:10.775
of these manifolds.

00:36:10.775 --> 00:36:11.801
- [Audience Member] Cool.

00:36:11.801 --> 00:36:14.070
And also, do you happen to look into,

00:36:14.070 --> 00:36:15.840
since you were looking at
topology of that manifold,

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:17.940
did you look for non-trivial homology?

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:21.060
Do any topological analysis?
Are there non-trivial cycles?

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:22.650
Like in those things
that are branching out?

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:25.500
- Yeah, no, thus far, yeah.

00:36:25.500 --> 00:36:27.750
So thus far,

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:31.890
we've been able to characterize
only certain aspects

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:34.220
of the topology because
we're restricted to,

00:36:34.220 --> 00:36:36.570
we have about 1,500
neurons from the retina,

00:36:36.570 --> 00:36:38.880
about 1,500 from the cortex.

00:36:38.880 --> 00:36:43.880
And if we start to look for
things like persistent topology

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:46.350
and that other stuff,

00:36:46.350 --> 00:36:50.008
that induces an artificial
clustering on all of this

00:36:50.008 --> 00:36:53.040
by the way that neighborhoods are grown.

00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:55.920
So we actually experimented
some of those techniques,

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:57.633
but have not been using them.

00:37:01.392 --> 00:37:02.444
- [Audience Member 1] Thanks
so much for your talk.

00:37:02.444 --> 00:37:05.610
I had a question about
the kind of implications

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:07.290
or consequences of your results.

00:37:07.290 --> 00:37:09.270
So are you thinking of this

00:37:09.270 --> 00:37:11.970
as something about the architecture

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:14.040
of the neural network as deciding this?

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:15.630
Or is it something about the problem

00:37:15.630 --> 00:37:18.180
that's allowing the
architecture to take on

00:37:18.180 --> 00:37:21.600
this kind of retinal
neuron versus brain neural?

00:37:21.600 --> 00:37:25.346
- Right. Yeah, well,
they're coupled, right?

00:37:25.346 --> 00:37:29.130
The retina has a very
particular cyto architecture.

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:30.870
The cortex of the mouse

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:32.887
has a very particular cyto architecture.

00:37:32.887 --> 00:37:35.790
There are certain macroscopic details

00:37:35.790 --> 00:37:39.270
of the cyto architectural
organization of the mouse

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:44.270
that do not have certain
properties of advanced primates,

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:47.550
advanced mammals actually,
like primates or cats,

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:49.470
which are called orientation columns

00:37:49.470 --> 00:37:51.870
and other such activities.

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:55.320
So one of the technical
motivations for this work

00:37:55.320 --> 00:37:58.320
from the physiology side,

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:01.500
is in order to do the geometric processing

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:04.440
that has thought that orientation
hyper columns support,

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:06.840
you need the hyper column organization.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:08.880
And because that's lacking in the mouse

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:11.250
except at a crude statistical level.

00:38:11.250 --> 00:38:14.520
And what this implies is no,

00:38:14.520 --> 00:38:16.350
you do not need that organization,

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:18.750
at least at the level of
the first visual area.

00:38:18.750 --> 00:38:20.819
Of course, there's much
higher level stuff going on

00:38:20.819 --> 00:38:25.023
beyond what I talked about
here, but maybe next year.

00:38:27.030 --> 00:38:28.830
- [Priya] Any other quick questions?

00:38:30.452 --> 00:38:32.449
Okay, let's thank Steve again.

00:38:32.449 --> 00:38:35.616
(audience applauding)

